  "raw": " China. The earliest written evidence of its existence in highly detailed sketches of its internal architecture, in the late Tang Dynasty (AD 618-906), from which it was sacked in 1078 by the Song Dynasty when the then-conqueror Yuan Shikai led part of his army in massacre, and the city was afterwards rebuilt. It had very influential Mi community in the early part of its history. (See the List of ancient cities on Nanjing's Area Development of Public Welfare Department site.)\n\nThe city was a frontier (dongnan, in Chinese), from which it welcomed invasions. But it remained a center for safeguarding the clan culture between the clan administrators (maskshi) located in nuclei and the clan clansmen.\n\nIt was the clan capital for Jiangxi and the clan capital for the clan clansmen. Newly promoted clan craftsmen could have their equipment and personal skills (maskshi) acknowledged either in nuclei or clan clansmen",
  "serialized": "China. The earliest written evidence of its existence in highly detailed sketches of its internal architecture, in the late Tang Dynasty (AD 618-906), from which it was sacked in 1078 by the Song Dynasty when the then-conqueror Yuan Shikai led part of his army in massacre, and the city was afterwards rebuilt. It had very influential Mi community in the early part of its history. (See the List of ancient cities on Nanjing's Area Development of Public Welfare Department site.) The city was a frontier (dongnan, in Chinese), from which it welcomed invasions. But it remained a center for safeguarding the clan culture between the clan administrators (maskshi) located in nuclei and the clan clansmen. It was the clan capital for Jiangxi and the clan capital for the clan clansmen. Newly promoted clan craftsmen could have their equipment and personal skills (maskshi) acknowledged either in nuclei or clan clansmen",
